--- body ---

## Context

Claude Code writes native project memory into `projects/*/memory/` inside the
harness checkout, the same tree the nightly pull fast-forwards. Nothing commits
those writes. When an incoming commit touches the same path, the pull refuses
and keeps refusing, with no signal a person sees.

Observed 2026-09-29: padme's `claude-harness-pull` had failed every night since
2026-09-24 on 3 new and 1 edited memory notes plus two index lines, leaving the
host 134 commits behind — including the destructive-bash guard wiring. Rescued
by PR #1057 (3-way merge, union of index conflicts), byte-verified before the
local copies were discarded. The same afternoon doudou's primary checkout held
3 modified and 4 new memory files from other live sessions.

`idh sync` (0987) will name the blocking files, which makes the stall visible
but does not prevent it. The portable `~/.agents` clone contract in 0999
changes how the checkout is found, not whether memory writes are committed.

## Author decision: capture at roar

At roar time, automatically save and commit significant factual episodes to a
project-specific experience pool, ordered by date. These are souvenirs of what
happened, not extracted lessons. If no significant episode occurred, write
nothing. Roar must not propose rules or interpreted memories.

Capture both positive and negative significant experiences. Capture relevant
novelty, an evidenced expectation contradicted by observation,
material consequences for the task, or an actual conflict between prescribed
procedure and circumstances. Include near misses, prevented harm and
unexpectedly easy successes as well as failures and costly rework. Neither
positive nor negative outcomes receive automatic priority; significance is the
selection criterion. Record
specific context, event, observed outcome and evidence; do not invent agent
emotions or turn an episode into a general prescription. Selection requires
relevance judgment, but the saved account remains factual.

This is a literature-informed operational definition, not a psychological
consensus definition or a calibrated threshold. Research distinguishes novelty
from surprise and connects memory selection to priority and goals:

- Novelty and expectations: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8024513/
- Novelty versus surprise: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3858647/
- Arousal and priority: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3110019/
- Autobiographical memory and goals: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10789197/

Rule tension and observable task cost are engineering translations of these
mechanisms. Later editorial work may interpret experiences; it is separate
from roar capture. V8 fixes the journal layout under memory/journal/AAAA/ and the separation
between factual capture and topics; tracker 0909 owns integrated delivery.

Automatic local commits alone do not establish safe integration or preserve
fast-forward pulls: branch ownership, concurrent writes and cross-host sync
remain implementation questions. This decision is not a runtime change.

## Author direction: repository Markdown first

The shared cross-runtime contract is repository Markdown and AGENTS.md, with
no memory server, MCP dependency, embeddings or database. AGENTS.md points to
memory/MEMORY.md and relevant memory/topics/ bodies. Reading is instructed,
not claimed to be mechanically guaranteed across runtimes.

The proposed layout adds memory/journal/ dated factual experiences (roughly
300 words when needed), a short MEMORY.md index (roughly 100 lines), topics/
consolidated knowledge and a versioned DREAM.md consolidation prompt. The
earlier per-year, append-only journal decision is retained; for the
archival step, v8 records processing in dream reports without moving the
append-only source accounts or breaking their links.

A weekly systemd timer invokes one runtime non-interactively in an isolated
branch/checkout. Dreaming groups experiences, updates topics and the index,
and imports runtime-native memory as attributed source material. Native notes
are not authoritative project knowledge. Proposed crystallisation into
AGENTS.md, runbooks, tests or issues belongs in the reviewed dreaming PR,
never in roar capture. Adopted decisions retain their canonical records;
journal entries may reference them without becoming a parallel ledger.

The v8 design and tracker 0909 implementation train now record this direction.
This is not a deployed timer or a completed runtime change.
Project journal capture must still be committed and safely integrated without
dirtying or diverging the checkout used for harness pulls. Repository audience
and any private companion store need a concrete project-specific arrangement.

## Earlier options (capture trigger now settled)

1. Pull tolerates memory: rebase/autostash only `projects/*/memory/`, or merge
   with a union driver for `MEMORY.md` (index files are append-mostly).
2. Memory writes land as commits: a SessionEnd step (or `/lair`) commits and
   opens a memory-only PR, as the 2026-09-29 rescue did by hand.
3. Memory leaves the pulled tree, with its own sync. This is a memory ownership
   decision, not part of the repository portability work in 0999.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A host whose sessions wrote memory since the last pull still fast-forwards the harness the next night, in a disposable-HOME reproduction of the padme case (positive control: today's script refuses)
- [ ] No memory note is lost or duplicated across the reproduction (byte comparison of the note set before and after)
- [ ] A pull that still cannot proceed is reported where the author sees it (session start or `idh status`), not only in a journal
- [ ] `make check` passes
